## Review

I checked the program against each requirement:

- **R1** — `main` spawns exactly two workers, `t1` and `t2`. ✔
- **R2** — `a` and `b` are `Mutex`es shared via `Arc`, so a lock is held by at most one worker at a time. ✔
- **R3** — Both guards (`_ga`, `_gb`) are alive simultaneously during the critical section. Note that `_`-prefixed bindings still own their values, so the guards are not dropped early. ✔
- **R4** — `Mutex::lock()` blocks until the lock is free, then proceeds. ✔
- **R5** — Both workers acquire locks in the same global order (a, then b). A circular wait (each holding one lock and waiting on the other) is impossible under a consistent lock ordering, so deadlock cannot occur. ✔
